The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ptep_zap_softleaf_entry(mm, softleaf_from_pte(*ptep));
pte_clear(mm, vmaddr, ptep);
```
Reason:
These two lines were executed unconditionally when `pte_swap(*ptep)` was true, without checking the `pgste` flags. This allowed zapping and clearing a swapped PTE that could still be in use by the guest, leading to guest memory corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if ((pgstev & _PGSTE_GPS_USAGE_MASK) == _PGSTE_GPS_USAGE_UNUSED ||
    (pgstev & _PGSTE_GPS_ZERO)) {
	ptep_zap_softleaf_entry(mm, softleaf_from_pte(*ptep));
	pte_clear(mm, vmaddr, ptep);
}
```
Reason:
The added condition ensures that the PTE is only zapped and cleared if the page is marked as unused (`_PGSTE_GPS_USAGE_UNUSED`) or zero (`_PGSTE_GPS_ZERO`). This prevents clearing a swapped entry that is still in use, thereby avoiding memory corruption.

